This PR makes the remove modal and remove notification message handlers call `removeModal()` and `removeNotification()` asynchronously. Without this change, it's possible that when both `showModal()` and `removeModal()` are called sequentially, the previously synchronous `removeModal()` call might have happened before the `showModal()` call, resulting in a modal that doesn't go away on its own as expected.

The modal use case is particularly difficult if the modal blocks access to the rest of the UI and does not enable a way to close the modal. See https://github.com/posit-dev/py-shiny/issues/1318#issuecomment-2085437403 for further details.

We have a similar discrepancy with `shiny-insert-ui`/`shiny-remove-ui` and `shiny-insert-tab`/`shiny-remove-tab`, but it's less clear to me that this assymetry would cause any problems.

<details>
<summary>Example app</summary>

```r
library(shiny)

run_model <- function(delay=10.0) {
  start_time <- Sys.time()
  while (difftime(Sys.time(), start_time, units="secs") < delay) {
    # do nothing, just wait
  }
  return(Sys.time())
}

ui <- fluidPage(
  actionButton("run", "Run Model"),
  textOutput("results")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  v_results <- reactiveVal()

  observeEvent(input$run, {
    showModal(modalDialog(
      title = "Running model",
      "The model is running, please wait.",
      footer = NULL,
      easyClose = FALSE
    ))

    # adjust the delay in seconds down to zero
    results <- run_model(delay=0)

    v_results(results)
    removeModal()
  })

  output$results <- renderText({
    v_results()
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

