This PR introduces two big changes to the old, undocumented, but widely used `Shiny.onInputChange` API:

1. The function is now called `Shiny.setInputValue`; `onInputChange` sounds more like an event listener, not a setter. (The old function name will continue to work, but `setInputValue` will be the official, documented way to do it.)
2. You can pass a `{priority: "event"}` option for input values that want to be treated less like values to be monitored, and more like events that are not to be missed.

```javascript
Shiny.setInputValue("name", value, {priority: "event"});
```

Until now, Shiny has largely treated input values as a continuous stream of values that we sample at convenient times. If an input's value changed multiple times in quick succession, those changes [might be coalesced](https://github.com/rstudio/shiny/blob/cef1f3c7ee51269ada15b30613321475d7247d83/srcjs/input_rate.js#L184). If an input was set to the same value it already had, [it was a no-op](https://github.com/rstudio/shiny/blob/cef1f3c7ee51269ada15b30613321475d7247d83/srcjs/input_rate.js#L217).

#928 is a request to allow some inputs to be treated less like samples of a continuous stream, and more like discrete events, each of which is important. For example, clicking a point on a map might result in a `{lat, long}` input value being set; it's not only the lat/long values that are important, but also the fact that a click occurred at all. So we want two clicks at precisely the same location to always yield two distinct events, and for that to trigger an `observeEvent` on the server twice. But Shiny would see that the lat/long object is exactly the same the second time, and convert it to a no-op. In the past we've had to work around this by [adding nonces](https://github.com/rstudio/leaflet/blob/a4d20dae1a612463134e6b3201bb06757213002f/javascript/src/index.js#L142) to such values (which prevents deduplication/no-ops, but not coalescing--admittedly a much less common problem). This PR removes the need for that. It's not so much the client-side nonce-ing that's an inconvenience, more that the server side then needs to scrub that extra info away.

Previous to this commit, circumventing the client side dedupe logic was not enough. The
server side ReactiveValues object was also subject to deduping. With this commit, the
low-level ReactiveValues class's constructor now has a `dedupe` option, which defaults
to TRUE; the ReactiveValues used for a session's input has it turned to FALSE. I figure
if I had to work this hard to get the client to stop sending duplicates, and the input
values are only expected to ever be updated by the client, then there's really no reason
for server side deduping to be performed for this particular ReactiveValues object.

It would make sense as a future feature to also make deduping optional for user-created
reactiveValues and reactiveVal objects.

- [x] Get consensus with Shiny team on whether this is a good idea
- [ ] Add NEWS, documentation (where?)

