This adds `renderCachedPlot()`, which is like `renderPlot()`, but caches the plots to disk. The cache can scoped to a session, to one run of an app (essentially, one call of `runApp`, which can service multiple user sessions), or the app author can specify a cache directory and control the lifetime of the cache.

The base size for cached plot is 400x400 pixels. It will allow other aspect ratios, in multiples of 1.2 by default, although the multiplier can be changed. This means that it will create and cache plots at aspect ratio of 1:1, 1:1.2, 1:1.44, and so on, as well as 1.2:1, 1.44:1, and so on.

It will also cache different sizes, in multiples of 1.2 by default. So for the 1:1 ratio it will cache images at 278x278, 333x333, 400x400, 480x480, 576x576, and so on.

Minimal usage example:

```R
library(shiny)
shinyApp(
  fluidPage(
    sidebarLayout(
      sidebarPanel(
        sliderInput("n", "Number of points", 4, 32, value = 8, step = 4)
      ),
      mainPanel(plotOutput("plot"))
    )
  ),
  function(input, output, session) {
    output$plot <- renderCachedPlot({
        cat("renderCachedPlot user code\n")
        Sys.sleep(2)  # Add an artificial delay
        seqn <- seq_len(input$n)
        plot(mtcars$wt[seqn], mtcars$mpg[seqn],
             xlim = range(mtcars$wt), ylim = range(mtcars$mpg))
      },
      cacheKeyExpr = { list(input$n) }
    )
  }
)
```

