`abortOutput()` leaves the state of the output unchanged, unlike `req()` (clears it), `validate()` (grey message), or `stop()` (red error message).

And help me find a better name than `abortOutput()` please!

This is a dumb example, it just halts the output if the value is less than 10. I made an error throw if n > 80 to demonstrate that abortOutput will also preserve errors, not just valid values.

``` r
library(shiny)

ui <- fluidPage(
  sliderInput("n", "bins", 1, 100, 5),
  plotOutput("plot")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  output$plot <- renderPlot({
    if (input$n < 10) {
      abortOutput()
    } else if (input$n > 80) {
      stop("boom")
    }

    hist(runif(100), input$n)
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)
```

